---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import AmeliaMechanism from "@/components/projects/AmeliaMechanism.astro";
import AmeliaBenchmarks from "@/components/projects/AmeliaBenchmarks.astro";
import { ameliaProject as project } from "@/content/projects/amelia";
import "@/styles/projects.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="amelia-torch — Multiple imputation"
  description={project.description}
  pageClass="playground-page"
>
  <article class="project-page shell">
    <header class="project-hero">
      <p class="project-kicker">Playground / Statistical computing</p>
      <h1 class="project-title">{project.title}</h1>
      <p class="amelia-subtitle">{project.subtitle}</p>
      <p class="project-lede">{project.lede}</p>
      <div class="project-links">
        <a class="button" href={project.repository}
          >View on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
        >
        <a href="#performance">Explore the results <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
      </div>
      <p class="project-note">
        An unofficial, experimental implementation by Sheng Wan. Evidence snapshot: September 2026.
        Full statistical acceptance is still pending.
      </p>
    </header>

    <dl class="project-metrics" aria-label="Project findings and scope">
      {
        project.metrics.map((metric) => (
          <div class="project-metric">
            <dt>{metric.label}</dt>
            <dd>
              {metric.value}
              <small>{metric.detail}</small>
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p class="project-note amelia-result-boundary">
      The native CUDA result is a measured gain within a continuous-data workflow. The complete R
      compatibility route did not outperform parallel original Amelia, and the tested Apple GPU
      route was slower than CPU.
    </p>

    <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="research-value">
      <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-kicker">The research problem</p>
        <h2 id="research-value">More than filling empty cells</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="project-two-column">
        <p>
          Replacing a missing observation with a single best guess can hide uncertainty. Amelia’s
          bootstrap–EM method produces several plausible completed datasets. Researchers analyze
          each one and combine the estimates, accounting for variation within and between
          imputations.
        </p>
        <p>
          That repeated work can become expensive in simulations and sensitivity analyses. My
          contribution is a Python/R implementation and evaluation framework: preserve the reference
          workflow, test different compute devices, and measure both useful speed and downstream
          inference.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="gpu-mechanism">
      <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-kicker">Inside the computation</p>
        <h2 id="gpu-mechanism">Where a GPU can help</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        EM repeatedly estimates missing values’ conditional moments and updates a joint mean and
        covariance. Grouping rows with the same missingness pattern turns much of this work into
        matrix operations—the part that a GPU can execute in parallel.
      </p>
      <AmeliaMechanism />
    </section>

    <section id="performance" class="project-section" aria-labelledby="performance-heading">
      <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-kicker">Measured performance</p>
        <h2 id="performance-heading">The answer depends on the workflow</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Compare CPU and GPU on the same machine, at the same precision, within the same execution
        path. The native and compatibility interfaces do different amounts of work; their times
        answer different questions.
      </p>
      <AmeliaBenchmarks />
    </section>

    <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="interfaces-heading">
      <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-kicker">Compatibility engineering</p>
        <h2 id="interfaces-heading">Three explicit execution paths</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="project-steps">
        {
          project.routes.map((route) => (
            <div class="project-step">
              <>
                <h3>{route.title}</h3>
                <p>{route.body}</p>
              </>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p>
        The compatibility work preserves more than a formula: random-number state across R and
        Python, variable transformations, original result objects and documented upstream edge
        cases. CPU float64 remains the default. Device and precision choices are explicit;
        unsupported accelerators fail clearly.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="quality-heading">
      <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-kicker">Statistical validation</p>
        <h2 id="quality-heading">Faster is useful only if inference holds up</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="project-two-column">
        <div>
          <h3>What the evidence establishes</h3>
          <p>
            The complete T4 native/reference benchmark retained all 126 calls and 630 imputations.
            Recorded convergence, observed-value preservation and held-out completeness checks
            passed.
          </p>
          <p>
            A separate Mac study completed 10,500 fits across five execution paths. It assessed
            pooled regression estimates and interval coverage, going beyond reconstruction error
            alone.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>What remains unresolved</h3>
          <p>
            The Mac study passed its missing-at-random checks, but not every prespecified
            missing-completely-at-random coverage criterion. CPU and MPS reached the same
            conclusions; successful fitting did not make the study an overall pass.
          </p>
          <p>
            Full CUDA inference validation remains incomplete. Broader missingness patterns,
            end-to-end profiling and complete compatibility acceptance remain future work. The
            project is a development snapshot, not a fully validated replacement for Amelia.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="project-note">
        The reports retain the original thresholds, uncertainty intervals and unsuccessful outcomes.
        No inference claim rests on timing results or fixed numerical examples alone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="sources-heading">
      <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-kicker">Reproducibility</p>
        <h2 id="sources-heading">Follow the evidence</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        The benchmarks use public UCI datasets: Covertype, Individual Household Electric Power
        Consumption and YearPredictionMSD. The repository records attribution, preparation rules,
        hashes and repeat-level results. Complete raw dataset archives remain outside Git.
      </p>
      <ul class="amelia-source-list">
        {
          project.sources.map((source) => (
            <li>
              <>
                <a href={source.href}>
                  {source.title} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
                </a>
                <p>{source.detail}</p>
              </>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="project-note">
        Amelia’s method and original implementation are the work of James Honaker, Gary King and
        Matthew Blackwell. See <a href="https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v045.i07"
          >Amelia II: A Program for Missing Data</a
        >. amelia-torch is not affiliated with or endorsed by the original authors. Software
        license: GPL-3.0-only.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .amelia-subtitle {
    margin: 0.65rem 0 1.5rem;
    max-width: 35ch;
    color: var(--accent-strong);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2.25rem);
    line-height: 1.25;
  }
  .amelia-result-boundary {
    margin-top: 1.15rem;
  }
  .amelia-source-list {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1.25rem 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 1.75rem 0;
    list-style: none;
  }
  .amelia-source-list li {
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .amelia-source-list a {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .amelia-source-list p {
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .amelia-source-list {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
